<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Second Taifa of Murcia</h1><p class="st-meta">1147 CE – 1172 CE · capital Murcia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom of Ibn Mardanish, the Wolf King, who ruled eastern al-Andalus for 25 years in alliance with Christian powers against the Almohads.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-mardanish">Muhammad ibn Mardanish</a>, of a family of Muwallad origin from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> region, took power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a> in 1147 amid the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a>, soon adding Valencia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaen">Jaén</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denia">Dénia</a> and other eastern towns to his domains.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Known to Christians as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-mardanish">Rey Lobo</a>, he made treaties with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Genoa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>, hired Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> and minted gold coins widely used in Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>. He built palaces such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castillejo-de-monteagudo">Castillejo de Monteagudo</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a> and kept a sophisticated court while fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After losing allies and territory, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-mardanish">Ibn Mardanish</a> died in 1172, and his sons submitted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a>, who married into his family. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a> later revived as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/third-taifa-of-murcia">Third Taifa of Murcia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1147 CE – 1172 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Iberia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:82.53%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1147 CE – 1172 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Murcia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Muhammad ibn Mardanish</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Andalusi Arabic, Mozarabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Spain</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of the Wolf King, Murcia of Ibn Mardanish</dd></dl></aside></div>
